\section{Dataset}

We construct the dataset from the `Fiction' dataset of the ROCStories corpus \cite{Mostafazadeh2017}, which contains 98,581 five-sentence `stories' (see \citet{Mostafazadeh2016} for a discussion of this genre) of the form: \textit{[P] was a [A]. One day, [B]. Instead of [C], [D]. As a result, [E].}

To obtain a dataset of single sentences with simple and direct continuations, we create two datasets:

\textbf{Instruction}: \textit{You are to select the correct answer from a list of four options.}

\textbf{Example}: 'the village of bricqueville is located in calvados, a commune in the normandy region in france.' The correct answer is 'The village of bricqueville is located in calvados, a commune in the basse normandie region in northwestern france.'

\textbf{Dataset Statistics}: The BERT-based model was trained on 50,000 samples of the simplified text data.

\item \textit{Cloze}~\citep{marcus1993penn}: Provide the original sentence as the input, and replace words with a ``[MASK]'' token. The goal is to predict these masked tokens based on their contextual information in the input sentence.

\item \textit{Next Sentence Prediction (NSP)}~\citep{devlin2019bert}: Similar to the Cloze task, the objective is to predict the relationship between a sentence pair (i.e., whether they are consecutive sentences).
